Findings from redteamlab.local:

. Domain: redteamlab.local
. DC: WIN-56JEHJD0RI3 (192.168.56.101)
. Users: Administrator, jdoe, svc_backup, asrep, john, doe, krbtgt
. svc_backup — SPN set (HTTP/iis.redteamlab.local) → Kerberoastable
. asrep — pre-authentication disabled → AS-REP Roastable
. jdoe — AdminCount:True (possible former privileged account)
. HR-Team group — jdoe is sole member

Key Concepts:

. SPN (Service Principal Name): a unique identifier linking a service to the account running it.
  Required for Kerberos service ticket issuance.
  Any account with an SPN is Kerberoastable by any authenticated domain user.
. Pre-authentication: the default Kerberos security control that prevents offline
  cracking of AS-REP responses. Disabling it enables AS-REP Roasting.
. AdminCount:True: flag set on accounts that are/were members of protected groups.
  SDProp manages these accounts' ACLs — a breadcrumb of past privilege.
. LDAP: the protocol used to query AD for objects, attributes, and relationships.
  The foundation of all AD enumeration.

Key Lessons:

. Enumeration order matters: ports → services → users → groups → SPNs → ACLs → attack paths
. A "dead end" finding (account with no useful privileges) is still a legitimate, reportable finding — and may become an attack vector at a later phase
. memberOf LDAP attribute excludes primary group (Domain Users)
  an empty result does not mean "no access anywhere"

Interview Explanation:

"AD enumeration lab established the full attack surface of the domain from a low-privilege
position — identifying Kerberoastable and AS-REP Roastable accounts,
mapping group memberships and SPNs, and identifying the domain's port/service
footprint before any exploitation began."
